<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michiel de Ruyter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1607">1607</a>–<a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Michiel Adriaenszoon de Ruyter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michiel de Ruyter was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> admiral, the most celebrated commander of the Dutch navy. During the Second and Third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-wars">Anglo-Dutch Wars</a> he won the Four Days' Battle in 1666, led the Raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-medway">Medway</a> in 1667, when the English flagship Royal Charles was towed away, and fought off the combined English and French fleets in 1672–1673. He died of wounds received in battle against the French off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1676.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michiel-de-ruyter.jpg" alt="Michiel de Ruyter" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Bol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bol,_Michiel_de_Ruyter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Michiel de Ruyter was born on 24 March 1607 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a> in Zeeland, the son of a seaman who became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of beer porters. He worked for a time at a ropewalk and was sent to sea as a boatswain's apprentice at eleven, the usual age for Zeeland boys. In 1622 he fought as a cannoneer under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-prince-of-orange">Maurice of Nassau</a> at the relief of Bergen-op-Zoom. He then rose through the merchant fleet to become a ship's master at thirty. He had little formal education but spoke fluent English and tolerable French. The name De Ruyter was probably a nickname, perhaps from the verb ruyten, 'to raid', in reference to his privateering.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-dutch-war">First Anglo-Dutch War</a> he joined the fleet as a commandeur under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maarten-tromp">Maarten Tromp</a>, after first refusing the post as others were better qualified, and commanded a Zeelandic squadron that convoyed merchant ships through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>. In 1655 he sailed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with 55 merchantmen to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade and ransom enslaved Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> on the Danish side, and was knighted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-denmark">Frederick III of Denmark</a> after liberating Nyborg in 1659.</p><p>As lieutenant admiral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-dutch-war">Second Anglo-Dutch War</a> he retook the Dutch West African posts in 1664, but his raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> in 1665 had less success. In 1672 an Anglo-French fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a>, and the Comte d'Estrées faced his fleet of 75 ships. At Solebay he aimed to damage the English ships so heavily they needed long repairs, and later he used the shoals as shelter and detached small squadrons to keep the reluctant French out of the main action. He thus prevented a landing. In 1674 his expedition to Martinique failed with 143 killed and 318 wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1676 a cannonball struck him in the leg during the Battle of Augusta, and he died a week later at Syracuse. His embalmed body went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, and on 18 March 1677 he received a state funeral and was buried in the Nieuwe Kerk, where a monument by Rombout Verhulst was completed in 1681. His sailors called him bestevaêr, 'grandfather', for his disregard for hierarchy and bold undertakings. He is credited with a part in founding the Netherlands Marine Corps, established on 10 December 1665, and six ships of the Royal Netherlands Navy have borne his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michiel de Ruyter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Commercial rivalry with England produced three Anglo-Dutch Wars (1652–1654, 1665–1667 and 1672–1674); in 1667 <b>Michiel de Ruyter</b> raided the Medway and towed away the English flagship Royal Charles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The grand pensionary Johan de Witt and Admiral <b>Michiel de Ruyter</b> established the Regiment de Marine of the Dutch Republic.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlissingen">Vlissingen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.45,3.58&amp;year=1651" aria-label="Vlissingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4631 382 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Michiel de Ruyter"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5081 652h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5081 652h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.45,3.58&amp;year=1651">What was here in 1651? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
